RUNG A -- SPARC gas-dominated discs: the only stellar-mass-free sample with RESOLVED kinematics
======================================================================================================================
  147 SPARC discs with a positive HI contribution at the outermost measured radius
       cut on f_* at the outer point    N  a_0 (1.33 M_HI ONLY)   a_0 (all baryons)  literal V_flat^4/G M_HI   <f_*>
                                 all  147             2.659e-10           9.010e-11                4.700e-10   0.552
                           f_* < 0.4   52             1.006e-10           7.564e-11                1.789e-10   0.228
                           f_* < 0.3   36             9.496e-11           7.787e-11                1.666e-10   0.182
                           f_* < 0.2   21             9.088e-11           7.816e-11                1.666e-10   0.146
                          f_* < 0.15   13             8.317e-11           7.075e-11                1.444e-10   0.107
  
  WHY THE LITERAL FORMULA IS AN UPPER BOUND.  v^4 = G M a_0 is exact only asymptotically and only for the TOTAL
  baryonic mass.  Three terms separate it from what the data actually constrain, and all three are computed here:
    (1) DISC GEOMETRY: at the outermost point the HI disc's in-plane force equals that of a point mass 1.33x M_gas (+0.124 dex), so the point-mass formula understates the baryonic pull -> a_0 too high by +0.155 dex
    (2) THE KERNEL IS NOT ASYMPTOTIC: median y = g_gas/a_0 = 0.0252 at that point, so v^4 = G M a_0 (1+sqrt(y)/2)^2 -> a_0 too high by +0.060 dex
    (3) V_flat vs the last measured point: +0.009 dex
    (4) and the item's assumption itself -- STARS OMITTED.  Global stellar share of these discs 0.164, local share at the point used 0.146 -> the gas-only answer is high by +0.086 dex
  
  literal v_flat^4/(G x 1.33 M_HI)                = 1.666e-10  <- the item's formula, an UPPER bound
  kernel at the outermost point, GAS ONLY         = 9.088e-11  +- 0.088 dex  (stars still omitted)
  kernel at the outermost point, ALL BARYONS      = 7.816e-11  +- 0.083 dex  <- best estimate
  predicted literal-minus-gas-only gap from (1)+(2)+(3) = +0.223 dex; observed +0.263 dex
    RUNG  A  SPARC gas-dominated, resolved (f_*,loc < 0.2) a_0 = 7.816e-11  +- 0.094 dex   (-0.08 canon, -0.16 alt)   21 discs, per-galaxy scatter 0.26 dex
  [PASS] 124.A the gap between the item's literal formula and what the data constrain is fully accounted for by three named terms -- the disc's in-plane force exceeding a point mass, the kernel not being asymptotic at the last measured radius, and V_flat not being the last point -- with nothing left over.  So the literal 1.7e-10 is not a measurement of a_0; it is the same number seen through a point-mass asymptotic approximation that these discs do not satisfy   (predicted gap +0.223 dex = geometry +0.155 + kernel +0.060 + V_flat +0.009; observed +0.263 dex; residual -0.040)
  [PASS] 124.A2 AGAINST INTEREST: rung A does not reach the item's 15%.  Its statistical error alone is 21% on 21 galaxies and item 103's calibration floor adds 10%.  The item's own criterion is missed by a factor of about two   (a_0 = 7.816e-11 +- 0.094 dex = 24%, target 15%)
  residual M/L sensitivity of rung A: the stellar term is 15% of g_bar at the point used, so d log a_0/d log Upsilon = -0.182 -- a factor-2 error in Upsilon costs 0.055 dex.  Not zero, but a sixth of the full deep tail's.

