     intrinsic axis ratio q0 = 0.4: ALFALFA slope +0.247 dex/dex, median a_0 8.43e-11 -- dwarfs being THICKER than q0 = 0.2 would push their a_0 DOWN further, i.e. the wrong way to explain the trend away
  [PASS] 124.B (the finding that reorganises three items) the HI LINE WIDTH is not a rotation speed at low mass, and the damage is measurable: a_0 = v^4/(G M_b) from ALFALFA W50 runs UP with baryonic mass at +0.25 dex per dex over three decades, where the same quantity from SPARC's resolved rotation curves rises 3.5x more slowly (+0.07, itself only 2 sigma from flat, and driven by the very lowest-mass discs).  Since stellar masses are known for both, this cannot be an M/L effect -- it is the estimator.  Any stellar-mass-free sample selected on gas fraction is also selected to be LOW MASS, so it inherits the whole trend   (ALFALFA W50 slope +0.246 dex/dex (a 0.74 dex swing over the range); SPARC resolved +0.070 +- 0.033 (+2.1 sigma from flat); ALFALFA a_0 = 2.23e-11 at log M_b ~ 8 against 1.41e-10 at ~11)
    RUNG  B  ALFALFA W50, log M_b > 9 (NOT M/L-free, shown for calibration) a_0 = 1.061e-10  +- 0.044 dex   (+0.05 canon, -0.03 alt)   11507 galaxies; the gas-dominated cut of the SAME data gives 6.45e-11
  Rung B is deliberately NOT entered as a stellar-mass-free measurement: its gas-dominated subsample gives
     6.447e-11, which is -0.15 dex from rung A, and check 124.B says why.

======================================================================================================================
RUNG C -- Leisman+2017 almost-darks (the item's own named sample), restated as an a_0
======================================================================================================================
  112 almost-darks with a usable width and inclination; median M_gas/M_b = 0.920
  a_0 from v^4/(G x 1.33 M_HI) = 6.210e-12 +- 0.116 dex; including the (small) stellar mass, 6.133e-12
  that is -1.18 dex from canonical -- a factor 15 low.  Item 31 reported this as -0.30 dex in log v; 4 x that is the number here.
  the SAME estimator on ordinary ALFALFA galaxies of the same baryonic mass gives 7.060e-11 (-0.12 dex from canonical): so -0.12 dex of the almost-darks' offset is the generic width systematic of check 124.B and -1.06 dex is differential, matching item 31's -0.265 dex in log v exactly (4 x it).
  Item 31b then showed that most of even THAT differential is ALFALFA's width-dependent detection limit: matched
  on the detection ceiling (flux and noise only, so not a collider) the almost-dark excess is -0.089 +- 0.033 dex
  in log v = -0.36 +- 0.13 dex in a_0, which would put them at 3.110e-11 rather than 6.133e-12.
  Both numbers are far below both footings.  Neither is a measurement of a_0; both are measurements of how badly
  an unresolved line width performs on a dwarf, which is what check 124.B quantifies on ordinary galaxies.
    RUNG  C  Leisman+2017 almost-darks (unresolved widths) a_0 = 6.133e-12  +- 0.319 dex   (-1.18 canon, -1.27 alt)   SYSTEMATIC-DOMINATED, not usable; see 124.B and item 31b
  [PASS] 124.C AGAINST INTEREST: the item's own named sample gives an a_0 more than a dex below both footings, and it must be reported as such: 6.1e-12, a factor 15 below canonical.  Only a small part of that is the generic width systematic of 124.B (0.12 dex); the rest is the differential item 31 measured and item 31b then traced mostly to ALFALFA's width-dependent detection limit, leaving -0.36 +- 0.13 dex in a_0 after matching.  Whichever number is taken, this rung does not measure a_0 and must be carried on the ladder as a failure, not dropped   (almost-darks 6.133e-12 (-1.18 dex from canonical); same-mass ordinary ALFALFA 7.060e-11 (-0.12 dex); differential -1.06 dex)

