  [PASS] M124a injecting a_0 = 4.0x canonical into rung A's own g_bar values must be recovered exactly   (injected 3.744e-10, recovered 3.744e-10 (-0.0000 dex))
  [PASS] M124b turning the kernel off (nu = 1) must make rung A's gas-dominated discs unfittable by any a_0   (nu = 1 returns a_0 = nan)
  [PASS] M124c the ALFALFA mass trend is not a sample-size artefact: random subsamples of the full catalogue the size of the most massive bin never reproduce that bin's a_0   (most massive bin 1.413e-10 vs random draws 1.026e-10 +- 0.010 dex (+14.3 sigma))
  [PASS] M124d shuffling which galaxy gets which HI mass must break the measured trend, confirming that it is a property of the pairing and not of the marginal distributions.  (The shuffled slope goes to 4 x d log v/d log M_b, since the denominator no longer tracks the numerator -- a different number, which is the point)   (shuffled slope +1.239 vs real +0.246)

======================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- ITEM 124
======================================================================================================================
  The item asked for a_0 to 15% from a sample with no stellar mass in it.  Four such samples exist in this
  repository and they do not agree with each other, so the honest answer is a ladder with one usable rung.

    A  SPARC gas-dominated discs, RESOLVED    7.816e-11 +- 0.094 dex   24%   <-- the only usable one
    C  Leisman+2017 almost-darks              6.133e-12                    a dex low
    D  Lelli+2015 tidal dwarfs                1.734e-11                    a factor 4 low

  WHY THEY DISAGREE, in one number: a_0 = v^4/(G M_b) measured from ALFALFA HI line widths runs up with baryonic
  mass at +0.25 dex per dex over three decades, while the same quantity from SPARC's resolved rotation curves
  rises 3.5x more slowly from SPARC's resolved rotation curves (+0.070 +- 0.033, itself only 2 sigma from flat).
  Stellar masses are known for both samples, so this is not the M/L wall -- it is the
  line width failing to be a rotation speed in low-mass systems.  Removing the stars from a sample forces it to be
  gas-rich and therefore low-mass, so every stellar-mass-free catalogue built on unresolved HI inherits the whole
  bias.  That single fact accounts for item 31's almost-dark deficit (which item 31b had already traced to a
  related width-selection effect) and it is why rungs B and C cannot be used.

  WHAT SURVIVES.  On the 21 SPARC discs whose outermost measured point is more than 80% gas, with the neglected
  stars and the finite-radius kernel term both corrected, a_0 = 7.82e-11.  That agrees with item 102's
  M/L-free deep tail (7.4e-11) and with the KiDS dwarf lens stack (9.6e-11) inside 0.15 dex, all three sitting on
  or just below the canonical footing, with the alt footing above all of them.  It is 24%, not 15%.
  The class of measurement the item wanted -- a_0 with no stellar M/L at all -- exists and is self-consistent;
  what does not exist is a large enough sample of it with resolved kinematics.

